# Prettier is configured with `endOfLine: "lf"`, so a working tree checked out
# with CRLF fails `prettier/prettier` on every line of every file. Git's
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default) does exactly that, which made
# package-wide `pnpm lint` unusable on Windows machines. Pinning the checkout
# to LF keeps the working tree and Prettier in agreement on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the exception: the interpreter needs CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never touch binaries — the detection above is heuristic, these are certain.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tar binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
